Failed new results of Swedish asylum seekers' age assessment tests revealed
In Sweden, a test was carried out among asylum-seeking applicants, assessing their age with a novel approach medically; the most recent tests show that more than 80 per cent were rated older than 18, but not everyone believes in the accuracy of the results.
The Swedish National Forensiciencies Institute (Rättsmedicinalverket) has started the tests earlier this year.
The tests were set up for a more accurate assessment of their asylum application after the Swedish Migration Agency (Migrationsverket) was criticized for failing to correctly determine the age of a number of supposedly underage refugees.
So to date 2,880 cases have been put to the forensic tests and the forensiciencie institute has issued a total of 2481 tests between mid-March and July 31.
Eighty per cent (2002) were estimated to be 18 years old or older, and for a further 25, the forensiciencie Institute estimated that what is being tested "may be 18 years old or older."
In 432 cases it was concluded that a person "probably younger than 18" was "probably younger."
Tests will only be used if the a person's age is in question.
According to Johan Goranston, who works at the forensic medical institute, the results are consistent compared to those in the previous months.
"We don't see a big difference compared to the statistics in recent months, the numbers are quite consistent," he told The Local.
The fact that a large proportion of cases were rated older than 18 does not necessarily mean that asylum seekers have been lying about their age.
The institute issued 581 tests in May and said 442 cases "there are indications that the person under investigation is 18 years old or older."
But when the Svenska Dagbladet newspaper looked at the results of their 581 tests, it turned out that in 243 cases people had directly said they would get 18 this year.
This medical age assessment method, using X-road images of wisdom toothbrushes and knee lizard MRT after allowing dentistists and radiologists to analyze, has been criticized in some circles.
One skeptic is Claude Marcus, an endocrinologist at the Karolinska Institute, whose tasks include assessing human biological maturity to find out whether they underwent puberty sooner or later than usual.
'I've had good reason to think about it and I don't think the method of measurement we are currently using should.
Identifying biological age in this way is very uncertain.
'If we can't say for sure, we shouldn't pretend we can," he told Metro.
But Johan Göransson, from the forensiciencies at the forensiciencies, said: "In general, it's safe to see a child inaccuciably mated.
It's the most at about 10% if the person is just under 18.'
