Simon Stevens, chief executive of NHS England, hailed the move, which was made public on Thursday as a "hundred-dignity intervention," while the National AIDS Fund (NAT) described it as a "revolting moment."
Sexual health clinicians in London, Brighton, Greater Manchester, Liverpool and Sheffield are among the frontrunners to be among the first to start registering people, with clinicians expected to be introduced across England by April 2018.
Preliminary prophylactic (PrEP) is a hotbed for HIV drugs that are taken before intercourse in order to reduce the risk of being infected with HIV.
Clinical tests show that PrEP significantly reduces the transmission of HIV among people at risk of infection.
Getting PrEP before an intercourse means there's a sufficient amount of certain drugs in a person's body that prevent HIV from becoming infected.
Such medications can be taken each day or no later than two hours before intercourse.
Deficiants eligible for Medicaid include gay or bisexual marijuana, transgender people and people whose partners are infected with HIV and will not receive treatment.
