"We warmly welcome this announcement."
Ian Green, chief executive of the Terrence Higgins Foundation, said: "Now it's important to ensure that the clinical trial across the country gets off the hookup and that none of those at risk of HIV are sidelined.
Now, now that the PrEP Clinical Tridmous Medicine drug has procrastinated, we are on our way to protecting more than 10,000 people who are at risk of being infected with HIV.
Sharon Hodgson, shadow health secretary of public, said:: "The start of a clinical trial for the PrEP is a very welcome and very elecoma after months of delays and dragging the government's feet.
Evidence shows just how many changes this drug can lead to the predicament and the prevaluation of this life-changing infection.
