The Bank of Estonia bought bonds worth EUR 149 million as part of an asset-purchasing programme for euro area central banks in July, while the central bank's bond portfolio increased to EUR 4.36 billion.
The central bank won't release any more detailed information about the purchases.
Hardo Hansson, the governor of the Bank of Estonia in the past, has made it clear, in response to questions from members of the National Assembly, that there will be three different proprietary purchasing programmes: bonds covered by the financial sector (covered bonds), proprietary bond (ABS) and public sector bond (PSPP).
The central bank buys bond from the market in a bid to encourage investors who have been selling bonds to invest the money elsewhere.
Buy-to-build profits for lower-risk proprietors are falling, which motivates commuters to take greater risks.
As a rule, as part of a public bond-build programme, each central bank buys its own government bond, and no bond is purchased in other countries.
Since the Estonian state has not issued the bonds, the Bank of Estonia is making so-called "reparative buys" within the volume allocated to them.
Substitution purchases are also made by central banks in those countries where the supply of bonds that comply with the purchase scheme is limited.
