GMB concerned about investment in McVitie's Glasgow plant
The trade union is seeking reassurance from chef maker McVitie executives about long-haul production plans at its Glasgow factory.
According to GMB Scotland, the company has remained silent on the future of the Tollcross site.
More than 500 people are employed in the factory, which produces Hobnobs and Rich Tea biscuits and other products.
Pladis, the owner of McVitie, said they had invested more than 5 million plebiscus over the past few years.
They hired more workers and union chairmen "are the first ones to hear" when something changes.
In a letter to the company's chief executive, the GMB MP McVitie has appealed "disappointed concerns that the funding will not be used to modernise production lines."
GMB Senior Managing Director Drew Duffy said: "The fact that we don't get clarity on Tollcross's business needs and future investment plan is of deep concern for our members.
Several generations of families have worked here and the workforce is largely made up of people from the local community and those around Glasgow, an essential pillar of proper employment in an area with high levels of unemployment and social deprivation.
Glasgow's eastern part of Glasgow, in terms of jobs, wages and well-earned earnings, and against the backdrop of Brexit, rising living costs and chronic declines in manufacturing, McVitie is desperately in need of a hard-earned earnings."
A spokeswoman for Place said: "The site manager only received a letter from GMB Scotland yesterday and the company responds directly to it as we value active communication with workers and trade union representatives.
Two months ago there was a regular meeting between staff and GMB representatives in Tollcross, where a number of issues were openly discussed.
At the same time, we discussed with our employees that, like all other companies operating in an extremely competitive and uncertain economic climate, we are reviewing our activities on an ongoing basis - and this applies to all of our companies and sites - to ensure their competitiveness.
He added: "In recent years we have invested more than 5 million pleboughnuncies into the Tollcross site, most of which have gone into production lines and improving operations.
"We fully understand Tollcross's legacy as a production site and if there are any changes to the activity of the site, the workers and the representatives of the union will be the first to hear about it."
